## Amethystora: the last word in /etc/audit/rules.d.
##
## augenrules concatenates every .rules file in this directory in name order and loads the result, so
## this file sorts after all the others on purpose: what it does is close the rule set. 08-hardening.sh
## makes sure nothing the audit package ships sorts after it, and 20-tests.sh fails the build if
## anything does.

## Never make the desktop wait for the audit subsystem. If the kernel's audit backlog fills up, the
## default is to block the process that caused the event for up to a minute, which on a machine with a
## watch on /dev/input means the whole session stops while somebody types. Zero drops the event
## instead. An audit record lost under load is a smaller price than a machine that appears to hang,
## and the events this image watches for are rare enough that it should not come up at all.
--backlog_wait_time 0

## Immutable rules. Anything that reaches root can still turn auditing off, but only by rebooting the
## machine to do it, and a reboot is itself an event. Without this, `auditctl -D` erases every watch
## above in one command and leaves nothing behind to say that it happened.
##
## The cost, and it is the only one: changing a rule now needs a reboot rather than `augenrules --load`.
## The rules on this machine ship with the image and are regenerated before auditd starts at every
## boot, so nothing does that except somebody debugging the rules themselves.
-e 2
